A||||16||Richard Parnell||V8 loading down on turns||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||08-14-2000||11:50 PM||||I have a question about the v8 engine in a Fiero.  Mine should be done in 2-3 weeks.  My friend has 2 Fieros w/V8s and he says he had been having a problem with the carb loading down badly on turns.  His solution was to build a plate to allow the carb to face forward in the engine compartment.  What do you other v8 Fiero owners know about this problem?||||reg||1
Z||000001||gixxer||08-15-2000||01:41 AM||||First off, find out what kind of carb(s) he has. On a Holley, you have the choice of side or center pivot bowls. You can also install vent "whistles" and extended pickups for the jets. I don't know much about the Edelbrock(AFB), but you can probably fab up some vent extensions or fiddle with the float level a bit.<BR>In either event, make sure the float level is pretty much set to spec and then work from there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Archie||08-15-2000||11:09 AM||||The problem is not in the direction of the carb.  It is in the Carb type.<P>Edlebrock, AFB's, Q-Jets & other carbs of that type have "wrap-around" fuel bowls and thus are pretty good at preventing fuel slosh (if the float level is set correctly).<P>Holleys are junk, they have no provision to limit fuel slosh and will throw fuel around no matter how you mount it.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||elfiero||08-15-2000||01:43 PM||||The best solution to this problem is<BR>ELCTRONIC FUEL INJECTION||||reg||6
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||08-15-2000||04:15 PM||||Thanks for the info on the carbs.  I'll talk it over with my friend.<BR>As far as the fuel injection goes, the main reason I'm going normal aspiration is to get rid of the computer's functions as much as possible.  I dislike all that computerized crap.  Gas and brakes is all you need.||||reg||14
Z||000005||batboy||08-15-2000||06:00 PM||||I have an Edelbrock Performer carb on my V8 Fiero and have no problems like what you are describing. As for Holley carbs being junk, well I'm not so sure about that. If Archie meant that they don't work well on a Fiero engine, then I'll have to defer to his knowledge on the subject. But, if he is referring to all Holleys being junk in all applications, then I'd have to disagree with that on personal experience. I have Holleys on a couple of my high performance Streetrods with no problems and used to have a dual line Holley 750 double pumper with mechanical secondaries on my big block Chevelle. This was back in my youth and I used to thrash the crap out of that poor car. Best carb I have ever run on a car too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||08-15-2000||06:44 PM||||I'd have to agree with archie about holley carbs being junk, with the exception of straight line driving. Get a holley slightly off camber, or bounce it around (off roading), or take it through a hard corner, and the float doesn't know what to do. Why is it that all the other companies can make a carb that handles these situations, but the biggest name in carbs can't? <P>I also agree with elfiero about going with the FI. The advantages of fuel injection far outweigh any disadvantages.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||13
Z||000007||fsrowsell||08-15-2000||07:57 PM||||Q-Jet... No Problems...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||gixxer||08-15-2000||08:05 PM||||I have to disagree with Archie and Rockcrawl. I've put Holley 4V carbs on several cars, autocrossed them both and NEVER had any fuel handling problems. <BR>It appears to me that a lot of people install them expecting a miracle drivability cure, and also to pick up that 40 HP that the guy at the parts counter said you'd get... and are disappointed. I've seen people crank up the float level to fix an off idle bog where slightly larger pump shooters or a different pump cam profile are indicated.<BR>When properly sized for the engine, a Holley<BR>is a very easy to set up and well behaved carb. <BR>Unfortunately, to set up ANY carb, it isn't enough to be a "carb man", you have to be an "ENGINE man" also! ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Terrybogin||08-15-2000||08:35 PM||||The experience that I have with Holley carbs is that they are inconsistant at anything less than full throttle. My son and I tried for months to tune a Holley for decent idle quality, emissions and part throttle economy. We bought a big load of jets, power valves, and air bleeds. The tuning was based on O2 mixture readings as well as a emissions station type smog sniffer. There was no "in between" on anything- it was either too rich or too lean. Most of the time, changing jets a couple of sizes did nothing, then going another size would drastically change the mixture. Most people would never notice these problems because they just run the carbs right out of the box and never go through the trouble of trying to super-tune the carbs. We finally gave up and put on a nice EFI. If we had done this to begin with, we would probably have saved money over what we spent on the carb plus all the tuning crap for it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Archie||08-15-2000||10:52 PM||||Hey guys!!!! ease up a little, I didn't mean to start a war.<P>I made the "Holleys are junk" post from work today and was working on several other things at the same time. I wanted to give Richard specific information as quickly as I could. The "Holleys are junk" was just a personal opinion of how Holleys compare to the other carbs. mentioned. I didn't have any idea that I could start a war so easily & I had no such  intent.<P>Wow, you guys take everything I say so seriously. Do you wanna hear what I really think about Holley?<P>Archie<BR>Home of V-8 Fiero engine conversions.<BR>More than 1 Million served.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||08-17-2000||06:26 PM||||Does anybody have any experience with the new Road Demon carb?  Its a 625 cfm, designed for 200-350hp cars.  I have a little hic up in the carb right now, from high vac to low vac transition.  Its an older carb, and I'm thinking of replacing it (I need an electric choke anyway, so weighing the issue of buying new choke for older carb or just buying new carb).  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">   <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||08-17-2000||11:39 PM||||Jeez Archie, ease up, nobody is flaming anyone in this thread. I merely agreed that a Holley might not be the best carb for a Fiero, but disagreed they're junk in all situations. It's ok to disagree with people if you have personal experience or facts to back it up. Everyone is entitled to their opinion. A civil debate is allowed. Name calling is not. From my experience with the older Holleys, they are the easiest carb to work on and to fine tune. I've had great success with Holleys in the past, but admittedly, most my cars were muscle cars used in straight-line competition.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||08-18-2000||11:55 AM||||Mellow out batboy, your taking everything I say to seriously.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000014||batboy||08-18-2000||12:48 PM||||I'm cool. It's just that with over 1000 members and who knows how many others lurking out there, I just want to make sure that the fine folks reading this understand that your statement was only one opinion and that some of us have a different opinion.<P>Actually what you said about the float chamber location does make sense if you're really cranking around a corner in a Fiero with a transverse mounted engine. Since I've had great luck with my Edelbrock Performer on my Fiero, I probably won't change. However, my 454 truck engine I'm building WILL have a Holley.||||reg||1
Z||000015||crazygt||08-18-2000||10:58 PM||||Hi there guys. Just want to give my two cents. The 4150 series Holley carb are a road race style carb. We use this Holley series carb for our V-8 conversions if you want one a lot cheaper use q-jet carb there just as good as the Holley<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||rprevo||08-18-2000||11:46 PM|||| All I know is I was a anti holley person<BR>untill someone that knew something about them<BR>showed me how to make a 750 cfm work<BR>on a transverse mounted 350 in my fiero<BR>and it doesn't slosh slur or druel<BR>in the corners or off the line.<BR>I thought Holleys were junk but lots of<BR>ignorance to the product causes tainted<BR>opinions!!<P>Ray||||reg||6
